{# Operator-facing diagnostic, so like contact_message.html.jinja it does not
   extend base.html.jinja -- the shared footer introduces the business to a
   customer, which is wrong for a message the operator sent to themselves.

   The relay details are in the body on purpose: when this lands in a spam
   folder weeks later, the useful question is "which relay sent it", and the
   answer should be in the mail rather than in a config file on a server. #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head><meta charset="utf-8"></head>
<body style="font-family: -apple-system, Arial, sans-serif; color: #1a2233; max-width: 480px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 24px;">
  <h2 style="color: #1a2b4c;">{{ business_name }}</h2>
  <p>If you are reading this, outbound email works.</p>
  <p style="color: #667085; font-size: 13px;">
    That means account verification and password reset will reach customers,
    which is what actually gates submitting a card.
  </p>

  <table style="border-collapse: collapse; margin: 20px 0; font-size: 13px;">
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 4px 12px 4px 0; color: #667085;">Relay</td>
      <td style="padding: 4px 0;">{{ smtp_host }}:{{ smtp_port }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 4px 12px 4px 0; color: #667085;">Encryption</td>
      <td style="padding: 4px 0;">{{ tls_mode }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding: 4px 12px 4px 0; color: #667085;">From</td>
      <td style="padding: 4px 0;">{{ smtp_from }}</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p style="color: #667085; font-size: 12px; border-top: 1px solid #e2e5ea; padding-top: 12px;">
    Sent from the admin panel. Delivery to the relay is not the same as
    delivery to an inbox -- if this reached spam rather than the inbox, check
    SPF, DKIM and DMARC for the sending domain.
  </p>
</body>
</html>
